+# Cordless Vacuum Comparison
+
+This comparison applies the corrected $400.00 purchase ceiling to every model that meets the retained feature and storage requirements. The two finalists are evaluated on the same packet-defined measurements, weighted formula, and two-year cost convention so the recommendation reflects the apartment’s actual floor mix and pet-cleaning needs.
+
+## Buyer requirements
+
+- Home: 750-square-foot apartment with mostly hard floors and two low-pile rugs
+- Pet: One shedding cat
+- Purchase budget: $400.00 before tax
+- Charging storage: No drilled wall mount
+- Required features: Sealed filtration; anti-tangle floor head; user-replaceable battery
+- Decision rule: Recommend the highest weighted score among every qualifying model at or below budget
+
+## Corrected shortlist
+
+Both finalists meet all three required feature tests and provide floor-standing charging storage that does not require drilling. Alder V9 stays $21.00 below the corrected ceiling, while Cinder Flex S sits $1.00 below it. The measurements show a compact-apartment trade-off: Alder V9 is 0.5 lb lighter, whereas Cinder Flex S offers six more minutes of standard runtime and 70 mL more bin capacity. Both carry a two-year warranty.
+
+| Model | Price | Weight | Standard runtime | Bin | Charging storage | Warranty |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|
+| Alder V9 | $379.00 | 6.3 lb | 48 min | 650 mL | Freestanding charge stand | 2 years |
+| Cinder Flex S | $399.00 | 6.8 lb | 54 min | 720 mL | Counterweight floor dock | 2 years |
+
+## Weighted scorecard
+
+Cleaning is the unrounded mean of the hard-floor, low-pile, and pet-hair ratings. Usability is the unrounded mean of runtime, handling, and dock ratings. Overall then applies the retained 40%, 25%, 15%, 10%, and 10% weights to Cleaning, Usability, Filtration, Noise, and Support respectively; rounding occurs only for the displayed two-decimal results. That method gives Alder V9 a narrow lead despite Cinder Flex S posting the higher Cleaning component.
+
+| Model | Cleaning (40%) | Usability (25%) | Filtration (15%) | Noise (10%) | Support (10%) | Overall |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| Alder V9 | 9.13 | 8.60 | 9.50 | 7.60 | 8.80 | 8.87 |
+| Cinder Flex S | 9.23 | 8.40 | 9.20 | 8.10 | 8.20 | 8.80 |
+
+## Two-year ownership cost
+
+The projection adds two complete annual filter sets to each pre-tax purchase price. It assumes no battery replacement during the two-year period.
+
+| Model | Purchase price | Two annual filter sets | Two-year subtotal |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| Alder V9 | $379.00 | $58.00 | $437.00 |
+| Cinder Flex S | $399.00 | $48.00 | $447.00 |
+
+## Recommendation
+
+Recommend **Alder V9** under the retained highest-weighted-score rule. Its 8.87 Overall exceeds Cinder Flex S at 8.80 by 0.07 point, and its projected two-year subtotal is $437.00 versus $447.00, a $10.00 advantage. For a 750-square-foot apartment that is mostly hard floor, Alder V9’s 9.4 hard-floor score directly fits the dominant surface. Its 6.3 lb operating weight and 8.4 handling score also favor carrying, turning, and storing the vacuum in a smaller home; both beat the runner-up’s 6.8 lb and 7.8 handling result. Its 9.5 filtration and 8.8 support ratings add useful margin for a home with a shedding cat. The choice is close, but the formula, lower projected cost, and apartment-oriented handling evidence all point the same way.
+
+## Trade-offs
+
+- Alder V9 leads on hard-floor cleaning, handling, dock score, filtration, and support; it is also lighter and has the lower purchase and projected two-year costs.
+- Cinder Flex S is stronger on low-pile and pet-hair cleaning, supplies longer standard runtime and a larger bin, and posts the quieter 8.1 noise score, so it remains appealing when rug and shedding pickup outweigh maneuverability.
+- Lab scores are comparative, not guarantees for every home. Prices are a snapshot, not live quotes. The cost projection excludes tax, shipping, electricity, unlisted consumables, and a replacement battery, which is not assumed within two years.
